### How Does the DFS with Backtracking Work?

The outer loop tries every cell as a starting point. From each start, the recursive DFS carries the current row, column, and the index into the word being matched. Three checks happen before exploring: bounds check, character match, and visited check.

To mark a cell as visited, we overwrite it with a sentinel character. After exploring all four directions, we restore the original character. This is the backtracking step.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DFSTrace {
    private static final int[][] DIRS = {{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}};

    public static boolean dfs(char[][] board, String word, int r, int c, int idx, int depth) {
        String indent = "  ".repeat(depth);
        if (idx == word.length()) {
            System.out.println(indent + "MATCH COMPLETE");
            return true;
        }
        if (r < 0 || r >= board.length || c < 0 || c >= board[0].length
                || board[r][c] != word.charAt(idx)) {
            return false;
        }
        System.out.println(indent + "Visit (" + r + "," + c + ") = '" + board[r][c] + "'");
        char orig = board[r][c];
        board[r][c] = '#';
        for (int[] d : DIRS) {
            if (dfs(board, word, r + d[0], c + d[1], idx + 1, depth + 1)) {
                board[r][c] = orig;
                return true;
            }
        }
        board[r][c] = orig;  // backtrack
        System.out.println(indent + "Backtrack (" + r + "," + c + ")");
        return false;
    }
}
```

### How Is This Different from Word Search II?

| Aspect | Word Search (single word) | Word Search II (multiple words) |
|---|---|---|
| Input | One word to find | A list of words to find |
| Approach | DFS per starting cell for one word | Trie + DFS: one sweep for all words |
| Pruning | Character mismatch only | Trie prefix prunes entire branches |
| Efficiency | O(m*n*4^L) per word | O(m*n*4^L) total for all words |
| Collection | Return boolean | Collect all found words |

```java
// Word Search: check one word
if (dfs(board, word, r, c, 0)) return true;

// Word Search II: walk Trie node during DFS
TrieNode next = node.children.get(board[r][c]);
if (next == null) return;  // Trie prunes
```

### What Are the Edge Cases?

Edge cases include: a word longer than the total number of cells (impossible), a single-cell grid, a word that requires using the same cell twice (impossible by rules), and a board where every cell is the same letter.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WordSearchEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Single cell board
        char[][] single = {{'A'}};
        System.out.println(WordSearch.exist(single, "A"));   // true
        System.out.println(WordSearch.exist(single, "AB"));  // false

        // All same letters
        char[][] same = {{'A', 'A'}, {'A', 'A'}};
        System.out.println(WordSearch.exist(same, "AAAA"));  // true (path exists)
        System.out.println(WordSearch.exist(same, "AAAAA")); // false (only 4 cells)
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
true
false
```

### What Are Common Mistakes?

The first common mistake is forgetting to restore the character after backtracking. This causes cells to stay permanently marked, and valid words get rejected.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: never restores the cell
board[r][c] = '#';
boolean found = dfs(board, word, r + 1, c, index + 1);
return found;  // BUG: board[r][c] stays '#' forever
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: always restore
board[r][c] = '#';
boolean found = dfs(board, word, r + 1, c, index + 1);
board[r][c] = original;  // restore even if found is true
return found;
```

The second mistake is not short-circuiting. Once any direction returns true, you should return immediately without exploring the remaining directions.

### What About Time and Space Complexity?

The worst-case time complexity is O(m * n * 4^L) where m and n are the grid dimensions and L is the word length. From each cell, we branch into up to 4 directions for each character. In practice, the character matching prunes most branches quickly.

The space complexity is O(L) for the recursion depth, since we go at most L levels deep.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WordSearchComplexity {
    // Time: O(m * n * 4^L)
    //   m*n starting cells, up to 4^L paths from each
    // Space: O(L) recursion depth
    //   L = word length (max recursion depth)
    // In practice, much faster due to character mismatch pruning
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly follow up with:

- Word Search II: find multiple words using a Trie for prefix pruning.
- Can you solve this without modifying the board? Use a visited boolean matrix instead of overwriting cells.
- Optimize for a very long word: check if the board contains enough of each required character before starting DFS.
- Allow diagonal movement: add four more direction vectors to the direction array.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WordSearchOptimization {
    // Pre-check: count character frequencies
    public static boolean canContain(char[][] board, String word) {
        int[] boardFreq = new int[128];
        int[] wordFreq = new int[128];
        for (char[] row : board) for (char c : row) boardFreq[c]++;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) wordFreq[c]++;
        for (int i = 0; i < 128; i++) {
            if (wordFreq[i] > boardFreq[i]) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```
